## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/services/contentstack.ts** (`Stack`): This is where the Contentstack SDK Stack is initialized. The `options` object passed to `contentstack.Stack(options)` on line 69 lacks the `live_preview` configuration key required to enable Contentstack Live Preview. Without it, the SDK never subscribes to preview events and queries never include preview draft content — the entire Live Preview feature is inoperative from this point down. Every downstream query (getEntry, ContentstackFactory, useContent) inherits this gap.
  - **Minimal fix:** Add `live_preview: { enable: true, host: CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST }` to the `options` object (lines 55–67), where `CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST` is read from `process.env` alongside the existing env vars (add `CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST = ''` to the destructuring on lines 17–24). Then after `Stack` creation, call `Stack.livePreviewQuery(request)` where `request` carries the `live_preview` hash from the CMS iframe URL query params — this tells the SDK to return draft content for preview. The `ContentstackFactory` query methods already delegate to `this.stack` so they will automatically include preview data once the Stack is configured. The `ContentstackProvider` in `src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx` will re-render consumers because `useContent` → `getContentByKey` reads from `content` which is memoized on `defaultContent`; when the server-side data fetching includes the live preview hash, the context value updates and the UI reflects draft content in real time.


## Lessons From Prior Runs (persisted — a previous attempt at this or a similar story already hit these problems)
Any public method returning Promise<void> that wraps a Node.js EventEmitter callback (e.g. server.listen, child_process.spawn, stream.pipe) MUST:
1. Pass BOTH resolve AND reject to the Promise executor: `new Promise((resolve, reject) => {`
2. Wire the error event to reject: `emitter.on('error', reject)` — EADDRINUSE, EACCES, and other async errors are emitted on the EventEmitter, NOT thrown synchronously. Without `server.on('error', reject)`, a port-collision hangs the Promise forever. 3. Validate inputs in the constructor BEFORE the Promise is created. TypeScript types are compile-time only; at runtime port=NaN/Infinity/undefined bypasses them. Use `Number.isInteger(port) && port >= 0 && port <= 65535` and throw RangeError if invalid. VERIFIABLE: `grep -n 'reject' src/index.ts` must return at least two hits — one in the executor signature `(resolve, reject)` and one in `.on('error', reject)`. MINIMAL CHANGE & REUSE. Make the smallest change that satisfies the requirement — fewer lines of code is always better. Before writing any new function or helper, search the existing codebase for one that already does the job (use the CodeGraph `helpers` tool when it is offered in your prompt) and reuse it by importing it; do NOT duplicate existing logic or introduce new abstractions/fields when a concise edit to existing code suffices. Acceptance criteria describe the behavior to satisfy — they are not a mandate to add new code paths. [Self-Heal] Always patch only the broken line range on a repeated syntax-error retry -- never regenerate the whole file, since a full rewrite tends to reproduce the same corruption elsewhere.

[Self-Heal] Review REPEATEDLY rejected AMSD-2041 (unresolved after 2 cycles) for:
- The options object passed to contentstack.Stack(options) does NOT include a live_preview configuration block. The prescribed root-cause fix requires adding live_preview: { enable: CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_ENABLE === 'true', preview_token: CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN, host: CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST } (conditionally spread, like fetchOptions is today). Without this, the Contentstack SDK never processes live preview data — every downstream consumer (useContent, ContentstackProvider, 236+ components) is starved of preview content. This is the root cause identified in the RCA and it remains completely unaddressed. No changes were made to this file at all — confirmed by reading the file: the options object contains api_key, delivery_token, environment, branch, and fetchOptions only.
- The file exports a bare createContext<IContentstackContext>({}) with NO Provider component, NO useState, NO useEffect, and NO onEntryChange/onLivePreview subscription. The prescribed client-side fix requires a ContentstackProvider component that uses useState(defaultContent) as the content source and a useEffect that subscribes to Contentstack live preview events (Stack.onLivePreview or window message listener per Contentstack SDK docs) and calls setContent with merged preview data. Without a reactive Provider, useContent (consumed by 236+ components) always returns the static SSR-provided default content. No real-time preview updates can ever reach any consumer. All acceptance criteria related to real-time/draft content display are unmet. Confirmed by reading the file — it is 25 lines, exports only ContentstackContext, no Provider.
- useContent calls useContext(ContentstackContext) but there is no reactive Provider component supplying a changing value. The hook will always return the static value passed at render time. Even if Contentstack live preview events fired, there is no state setter to trigger re-renders of the 236+ consumers. The hook cannot propagate live preview updates as-is. This is a consequence of the missing ContentstackProvider (blocker above). Confirmed by reading the file — it uses useContext(ContentstackContext) with no reactive source.
- No vitest unit tests exist for the Live Preview feature. The test/unit/ directory does not exist at all (confirmed via list_files — zero files returned). Review criteria require new features to have corresponding vitest unit tests mirroring the src/ path. At minimum, tests should verify: (1) Stack options include a live_preview block with enable=true and preview_token when CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_ENABLE is 'true', (2) ContentstackProvider re-renders consumers when a live preview event fires, (3) content state merges incoming preview data correctly, (4) the page renders without errors when preview provides invalid/errored content. I searched for test/unit/**/* via list_files and found zero files. I searched src/ for onEntryChange|onLiveUpdate|live-preview-utils|live_preview|LivePreview and found zero matches.
- ICreateQueryConfig, ICreateEntryConfig, and ICommonContentstackConfig interfaces were not updated with an optional live_preview parameter. The prescribed fix requires adding an optional live_preview field so that live preview hash data can flow through to Contentstack queries. Without it, queries will always return published content even if the Stack is configured for live preview. No changes were made to this file. Confirmed by searching src/interface for live_preview|ICreateQueryConfig|ICreateEntryConfig|ICommonContentstackConfig — zero matches for live_preview.

## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a content editor modifies a draft entry in the Contentstack CMS, the preview pane displays the modified (draft) content without requiring the entry to be published
- When a content editor saves a change to an entry in the CMS, the preview pane updates to reflect the new content in real time, without a full page reload
- When no draft edits are pending on an entry, the preview pane displays the currently published version of that content without error
- The preview pane renders content for the entry currently being edited in the CMS, and that content changes visibly each time the editor updates a field in the CMS entry editor

## Codeline-Specific Facts (real, curated gotchas for THIS codeline — read before assuming local tooling behaves like a fully-configured environment)
- Local dev/pre-commit hooks require real Contentstack env vars (CONTENTSTACK_API_KEY, CONTENTSTACK_DELIVERY_TOKEN, CONTENTSTACK_ENVIRONMENT, CONTENTSTACK_BRANCH) to be set — next.config.js throws 'Missing required environment variables' at import time if they're absent, which breaks tsc/next-lint inside the husky pre-commit hook even when the source change itself is correct.
